"All of this has a cost. A system in which every branch can say no tends to be slow, and it can stall when the branches disagree, a condition that news reports call gridlock. Critics since the nineteenth century have argued that dividing power also divides responsibility, so that voters cannot easily tell whom to hold to account. Defenders answer that the delay is deliberate. Justice Louis Brandeis wrote in 1926 that the separation of powers was adopted “not to promote efficiency but to preclude the exercise of arbitrary power.” Whether that trade is worth making is a real argument about how governments should be built, and the Design Debate card in this lesson sets out both sides.": "093cf6028806",
