"In 1788, while the states were deciding whether to ratify the Constitution, James Madison named the danger it was built to avoid. “The accumulation of all powers legislative, executive and judiciary in the same hands,” he wrote in The Federalist No. 47, “… may justly be pronounced the very definition of tyranny.” The thought was not his own. Forty years earlier the French writer Montesquieu had argued that liberty cannot last where the power to make laws, the power to carry them out, and the power to judge are joined in one person or one body. Madison called him “the oracle who is always consulted and cited on this subject.” The practical question was how to keep those three powers apart.": "7ad042934421",
"In 1788, while the states were deciding whether to ratify the Constitution, James Madison named the danger it was built to avoid. “The accumulation of all powers legislative, executive and judiciary in the same hands,” he wrote in The Federalist No. 47, “… may justly be pronounced the very definition of tyranny.” The thought was not his own. Forty years earlier the French writer Montesquieu had argued that liberty cannot last where the power to make laws, the power to carry them out, and the power to judge are joined in one person or one body. Madison called him “the oracle who is always consulted and cited on this subject.” The practical question was how to keep those three powers apart. The first answer was separation. The Constitution gives the legislative power to Congress in Article I, the executive power to a President in Article II, and the judicial power to the courts in Article III. Each branch is chosen in a different way and for a different length of time: representatives for two years, senators for six, a President for four, and federal judges for as long as they show “good Behaviour.” The branches are also kept apart in their membership. No one who holds a federal office may sit in Congress at the same time, so a cabinet secretary cannot also be a senator. In a parliamentary system, by contrast, the ministers who run the government are usually members of the legislature. Separation alone was not expected to hold. Madison warned in The Federalist No. 48 that “power is of an encroaching nature,” and he doubted that boundaries written into a document, which he called “parchment barriers,” would stop a determined branch. So the Constitution adds a second device. Rather than sealing the branches off from one another, it gives each a limited share in the work of the others. The President takes part in lawmaking through the veto. The Senate takes part in appointments. Congress can remove a President or a judge. This arrangement is called checks and balances. Madison argued that it keeps faith with Montesquieu, whose rule forbids one branch to hold the whole power of another. Lawmaking shows how the checks work. A bill must pass both the House and the Senate, which is already a check inside Congress. It then goes to the President, who may sign it or return it with objections. That return is the veto, although the word never appears in the Constitution. Congress can still make the bill law if two thirds of each house vote to override. That is hard to do. According to the Senate's records, presidents have vetoed 2,599 bills and joint resolutions since 1789. More than a thousand were pocket vetoes, which cannot be overridden; of the 1,533 others, Congress has overridden 112. The first veto came in 1792, and the first override not until 1845. Other checks run through appointments and money. The President chooses ambassadors, department heads, and federal judges, but only “by and with the Advice and Consent of the Senate,” and a treaty needs the agreement of two thirds of the senators present. The Senate has voted down a nomination to the Supreme Court twelve times, the first of them in 1795. Congress also holds what is often called the power of the purse. In the words of Article I, “No Money shall be drawn from the Treasury, but in Consequence of Appropriations made by Law.” A President commands the armed forces and directs the departments, but cannot lawfully spend money that Congress has not approved. The most drastic check is removal. The House of Representatives may impeach, that is, bring formal charges against, the President, other civil officers, and judges for “Treason, Bribery, or other high Crimes and Misdemeanors.” The Senate then holds a trial, and conviction requires two thirds of the members present. Removal has been rare. As of 2026 the House has impeached officials twenty-two times, and the Senate has convicted eight of them, every one a federal judge. No President has been removed in this way. In 1868 the vote in the Senate to convict President Andrew Johnson was 35 to 19, one vote short of two thirds, so he was acquitted. The courts hold a check that the Constitution does not spell out. In Marbury v. Madison, decided in 1803, Chief Justice John Marshall wrote: “It is emphatically the province and duty of the judicial department to say what the law is.” The same opinion reasoned that a law contrary to the Constitution is void. This power, called judicial review, lets courts refuse to apply laws and executive actions that violate the Constitution. Yet the courts are checked as well. Judges are nominated by the President and confirmed by the Senate, and Congress decides how many justices sit on the Supreme Court. As Alexander Hamilton observed, the judiciary has “neither FORCE nor WILL, but merely judgment”: it depends on the executive branch to carry out its decisions. All of this has a cost. A system in which every branch can say no tends to be slow, and it can stall when the branches disagree, a condition that news reports call gridlock. Critics since the nineteenth century have argued that dividing power also divides responsibility, so that voters cannot easily tell whom to hold to account. Defenders answer that the delay is deliberate. Justice Louis Brandeis wrote in 1926 that the separation of powers was adopted “not to promote efficiency but to preclude the exercise of arbitrary power.” Whether that trade is worth making is a real argument about how governments should be built, and the Design Debate card in this lesson sets out both sides.": "f50edb26015b",
